what is percentile latency:
⚙️ 1️⃣ What is Percentile Latency?

Percentile latency tells you how long most requests take, not just the average.

Instead of showing one number (like average = 100 ms), you measure how fast 50%, 95%, or 99% of your requests are served.

| Percentile               | Meaning                                 | Example                                                 |
| ------------------------ | --------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| **P50 (Median)**         | 50% of requests finish within this time | If P50 = 100 ms → Half of requests ≤ 100 ms             |
| **P90**                  | 90% of requests finish within this time | If P90 = 300 ms → 10% slower than 300 ms                |
| **P95**                  | 95% of requests finish within this time | If P95 = 500 ms → 5% of requests > 500 ms               |
| **P99**                  | 99% of requests finish within this time | If P99 = 800 ms → 1% of requests are slower than 800 ms |
| **P99.9 (Tail latency)** | 99.9% complete within this time         | Extreme tail — often due to GC, DB, network, etc.       |

🧠 2️:Why Percentiles Matter (and Not Just Average)

The average latency can be misleading because a few very slow requests can distort it.

Example:Request latencies (ms): [100, 110, 95, 105, 120, 2000]
Average = (100 + 110 + 95 + 105 + 120 + 2000) / 6 = 421 ms
→ Looks bad 😬

But P95 = 120 ms → 95% of requests are actually fast!
Only 1 request was an outlier (2000 ms).

📌 So percentile latency helps you focus on real user experience rather than being skewed by rare spikes.

⚡ 3️⃣ Real Example in Microservices or Kafka

Let’s say your Kafka consumer processes 100,000 messages per minute.

If:

P50 = 100 ms

P95 = 400 ms

P99 = 1000 ms

Then you can interpret it as:

50% of messages are processed in under 100 ms (great 👍)

95% in under 400 ms (acceptable 👌)

1% (P99) taking up to 1 s (problematic tail 😬 —
maybe due to GC pause, DB lag, or network spike)


🔍 4️⃣ How Percentiles Are Calculated

Imagine you have a list of latencies:[50, 60, 70, 80, 100, 120, 130, 150, 500, 1000]
Step 1: Sort ascending (already sorted).
Step 2: Calculate percentile position:

Position = (Percentile / 100) * (N + 1)
Example for P95 (10 samples):
= 0.95 * (10 + 1) = 10.45 → between 10th and 11th value
So P95 ≈ 1000 ms (last value).

If you had thousands of values, the P95 would represent the
latency where only 5% of requests are slower.

💡 5️⃣ Why P95 and P99 Are So Important in Interviews & SLAs
| Concept   | Reason                                                                  |
| --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **P95**   | Used for performance SLAs — ensures 95% of users have a good experience |
| **P99**   | Represents tail latency — ensures system stability under heavy load     |
| **P99.9** | Used in high-scale, low-latency systems (e.g., trading, payments)       |

“P95 latency ≤ 300 ms for 95% of API calls.”
This means: out of 100 requests, only 5 can take longer than 300 ms.

