Important Interview question:1
🚀 Goal:what changes will you do in order to change increase the throughput

👉 Increase throughput = number of messages processed per second
Throughput depends on:

Producer efficiency (how fast it can send)

Broker configuration (how fast it can store)

Consumer efficiency (how fast it can read and process)

We’ll focus on Producer side and Consumer side tuning,
since brokers are usually managed separately (in cloud or cluster setups).

⚙️ 2:PRODUCER SIDE OPTIMIZATION
🧠 Key Goal:

Reduce network overhead, batch more messages, and tune acknowledgements.

A) Increase batch.size(implement batching)

Kafka producer groups multiple records sent to the same partition into batches.
Bigger batch = fewer network calls.
batch.size=65536  # 64 KB or higher
📈 Example:
If each record = 1 KB, a batch of 64 KB → sends 64 records in one go.
Reduces network trips → increases throughput.

(B) Increase linger.ms

Producer waits this much time before sending a batch, allowing more messages
to accumulate.
Slightly increases latency but boosts throughput.
linger.ms=10   # waits 10 ms before sending
💡 Tip: Start with 5–10 ms.
Good for use cases where low latency is not critical.

(C) Use Compression

Reduces payload size → less network I/O.
compression.type=snappy   # or lz4 or zstd

(D) Tune acks and retries

acks=1 → Leader acknowledgment only → faster but less durable
acks=all → Slower but safe
For throughput: choose based on reliability needs.
acks=1
retries=3

📊 Trade-off:
If reliability is less critical (e.g., telemetry data), use acks=1.
If critical, use acks=all but compensate with batching and async sends.

(F) Use Asynchronous Sending

Avoid blocking calls like:
producer.send(record).get();  // BAD — blocks until ack

Instead use:
producer.send(record, callback);  // GOOD — async
This keeps the pipeline always busy

(G) Use Multiple Threads / Producers

Each thread can have its own producer instance.
Parallel publishing increases total throughput.


