| Setting                                 | Typical Value | Effect                          |
| --------------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------- |
| `batch.size`                            | 32KB–128KB    | Larger batches → fewer requests |
| `linger.ms`                             | 5–20ms        | Adds delay → better batching    |
| `compression.type`                      | snappy / lz4  | Reduces payload size            |
| `acks`                                  | 1             | Faster, less reliable           |
| `buffer.memory`                         | 32MB–128MB    | Prevents producer blocking      |
| `max.in.flight.requests.per.connection` | 5–10          | More parallel requests          |


🧩 3️⃣ CONSUMER SIDE OPTIMIZATION
🧠 Key Goal:

Consume and process more messages per poll and parallelize processing.

(A) Increase fetch.min.bytes and fetch.max.wait.ms

Wait for more data to accumulate before sending to consumer.
fetch.min.bytes=50000       # wait for at least 50 KB
fetch.max.wait.ms=20        # or wait 20ms if less data
This helps amortize the network overhead.

(B) Increase max.poll.records

Number of records returned in a single poll.
max.poll.records=5000
📈 Example:
Instead of processing 500 messages per poll → process 5000 → fewer poll calls.

(C) Tune Consumer Processing Parallelism

Each consumer group can have up to one consumer per partition.
So to increase throughput:
Increase number of partitions in the topic.
Increase number of consumer instances (pods).

✅ Rule of Thumb:# of partitions >= # of consumers
Example:
If you have 8 partitions → scale up to 8 consumer pods.

(E) Commit Offsets in Batches
Instead of committing every record:
consumer.commitSync();  // after processing each record ❌

Batch commit:
if (count % 1000 == 0) {
    consumer.commitAsync();  // every 1000 messages ✅
}
Reduces coordination overhead with broker.

