🔁 When Does a Rebalance Happen?

1️⃣ A new consumer joins the group.
2️⃣ A consumer leaves (crash, timeout, manual shutdown).
3️⃣ A topic changes (new partitions added).
4️⃣ The subscription list changes (consumer subscribes to a new topic).
5️⃣ Session timeout occurs (consumer fails to send heartbeats in time).

🧠 Rebalance Algorithm Overview

Kafka uses partition assignment strategies, also known as Partition Assignors.
These define how partitions are redistributed when the group changes.

1️⃣ Range Assignor

Default in older Kafka versions

Assigns consecutive ranges of partitions to each consumer.

Example:

6 partitions, 2 consumers →

C1 gets [0,1,2]

C2 gets [3,4,5]

✅ Simple and fast

❌ Can cause imbalance when partitions aren’t evenly divisible

2️⃣ RoundRobin Assignor

Assigns partitions in a round-robin fashion.

Example:

6 partitions, 2 consumers →

C1 gets [0,2,4],

C2 gets [1,3,5]

✅ More balanced than Range

❌ Can cause more shuffling during rebalances

3️⃣ Sticky Assignor

Introduced in Kafka 2.4+, default since Kafka 3.0

Goal: Keep as many existing assignments as possible during rebalance.

✅ Reduces churn

✅ Preserves cache locality (helps low latency)

✅ Fair distribution

🧮 Algorithm logic:

Try to reuse previous assignments (stickiness).

Assign new partitions only to unassigned consumers.

Reassign only if needed to balance the load.

📈 Result:

Fewer partition movements.

Faster rebalances.

Better throughput and lower latency.


🧮 Simplified Example of Sticky Assignor

Scenario:
Topic = "orders"
Partitions = [P0, P1, P2, P3]
Consumers = [C1, C2]

Before:

C1 → [P0, P1]

C2 → [P2, P3]

Now C3 joins.

Rebalance Goal: Even out partitions

Total = 4 partitions, 3 consumers → each ~1-2 partitions

Sticky assignor keeps as many old assignments as possible.

After:

C1 → [P0] (kept)

C2 → [P2, P3] (kept)

C3 → [P1] (new assignment)

✅ Only P1 moved → minimal disruption



4️ Cooperative Sticky Assignor (Incremental Cooperative Rebalance)

Introduced in Kafka 2.4+.

Improves further: avoids full-stop-the-world rebalances.

Consumers gradually revoke and assign partitions incrementally.

⚙️ Algorithm Steps:

When a new consumer joins, only the needed partitions are reassigned.

Existing consumers continue consuming unaffected partitions.

Consumers send PartitionRevoke and PartitionAssign requests incrementally.

✅ Benefits:

No full rebalance (less downtime)

Smooth scaling in/out

Perfect for large-scale production clusters






🕹️ Under the Hood – Rebalance Workflow

1️⃣ Join Group Phase

Consumer sends JoinGroup to the Group Coordinator.

Coordinator elects a leader consumer.

2️⃣ Sync Group Phase

Leader assigns partitions → sends assignment to coordinator.

Coordinator sends the final assignment to all members.

3️⃣ Heartbeat Phase

Consumers send periodic heartbeats to stay in the group.

If heartbeats stop → coordinator triggers rebalance.

⚡ Impact on Performance
| Strategy           | Rebalance Time | Partition Movement | Use Case                               |
| ------------------ | -------------- | ------------------ | -------------------------------------- |
| Range              | Fast           | High               | Simple cases, single topic             |
| RoundRobin         | Moderate       | High               | Multi-topic with even load             |
| Sticky             | Fast           | Low                | Production default                     |
| Cooperative Sticky | Very Fast      | Very Low           | **Best for large, dynamic clusters** ✅ |
